/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Set the theodolite at a station P, a horizontal distance D from the foot of the object, and sight its top T with a vertical angle α.
   - D is the horizontal distance in metres (not the slope distance along the sight). α is positive for elevation and negative for depression.
   - For sights beyond about 300 m add the combined curvature and refraction correction, 0.0673 d² m with d in km, to the computed height (the earth falls away below the horizontal line of sight, so the point is higher than D tan α shows).
   - When the foot of the object cannot be reached, D cannot be taped.
   - D is the horizontal distance from P to the vertical line through T. h₁ and h₂ are the heights of T above the instrument axis at P and at Q. d = (RL of axis at Q) − (RL of axis at P), positive when the axis at Q is higher.
   - The RL of T is found from P (axis RL + h₁) and checked from Q (axis RL + h₂).
   - Often no straight line towards the object is clear.
   - DP and DQ are the horizontal distances from P and from Q to T′. θ₁ and θ₂ are the horizontal angles at P and Q inside triangle PQT′.
   - The angle at T′ is 180° − (θ₁ + θ₂); a very small angle there makes D sensitive to angle errors, so the stations are chosen to give a triangle with angles between about 30° and 120°.
*/
