/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same top is observed by the double plane method from two stations P and Q 80.000 m apart, not in line with the tower. At P the horizontal angle QPT′ is 62°00′ and the vertical angle to T is 18°30′00″; at Q the angle PQT′ is 54°00′ and the vertical angle is 17°12′20″. The axis RLs are 101.625 m at P and 101.385 m at Q. Find the RL of T from each station. Take sin 54° = 0.80902, sin 62° = 0.88295, sin 116° = 0.89879, tan 18°30′ = 0.33460 and tan 17°12′20″ = 0.30966.
   1. θ₁ + θ₂ = 62°00′ + 54°00′ = 116°00′; the angle at T′ is 64°00′, a well-shaped triangle.
   2. DP = 80.000 × 0.80902 ÷ 0.89879 = 72.01 m; DQ = 80.000 × 0.88295 ÷ 0.89879 = 78.59 m.
   3. From P: h = 72.01 × 0.33460 = 24.094 m, RL of T = 101.625 + 24.094 = 125.719 m.
   4. From Q: h = 78.59 × 0.30966 = 24.336 m, RL of T = 101.385 + 24.336 = 125.721 m.
   5. The two differ by 2 mm, well within the reading accuracy, so the mean is taken: 125.720 m.
   Answer: RL of T = 125.719 m from P and 125.721 m from Q; adopted value 125.720 m. */
