/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The diaphragm of a tacheometer carries a stadia hair above and below the central hair, a fixed distance i apart.
   - D is the horizontal distance from the instrument's vertical axis to the staff, in m. f is the focal length of the objective, i the stadia hair interval, d the distance from the objective to the vertical axis.
   - An anallatic lens fitted between the objective and the eyepiece of an external-focusing telescope makes C zero, so D = 100 s directly.
   - On sloping ground the line of sight is inclined at θ.
   - θ is the vertical angle of the central hair's sight.
   - With the staff held normal to the sight instead, D = (K s + C) cos θ + h sin θ, a form rarely used now.
   - In the subtense method a horizontal bar of known length (2 m, with targets at its ends) is set up at the far station, square to the line of sight, using a small sighting device on the bar.
   - b is the bar length (2 m) and θ the horizontal angle between its targets.
   - In the tangential method the telescope has no stadia hairs.
*/
