/* book Worked example 2: Q: (a) A 2.000 m subtense bar at C subtends a horizontal angle of 0°52′40″ at A. Find AC. (b) In the tangential method, the vanes on a staff at E are 0.500 m and 3.500 m above its foot. The vertical angles are +6°24′ to the upper vane and +4°36′ to the lower. Find AE and the RL of E if the axis RL is 101.450 m. Take tan 6°24′ = 0.11217 and tan 4°36′ = 0.08046.
   1. (a) θ = 0°52′40″ = 3160″; θ ÷ 2 = 1580″ = 0.0076601 rad, and tan(θ ÷ 2) = 0.0076602.
   2. AC = (2.000 ÷ 2) ÷ 0.0076602 = 130.54 m. The small-angle form b ÷ θ = 2.000 ÷ 0.0153202 gives 130.55 m, the same to 1 cm.
   3. (b) s = 3.500 − 0.500 = 3.000 m; tan α₁ − tan α₂ = 0.11217 − 0.08046 = 0.03171.
   4. AE = 3.000 ÷ 0.03171 = 94.61 m.
   5. Height of the lower vane above the axis = 94.61 × 0.08046 = 7.612 m. RL of E = 101.450 + 7.612 − 0.500 = 108.562 m.
   6. Check with the upper vane: 94.61 × 0.11217 = 10.612 m; 101.450 + 10.612 − 3.500 = 108.562 m.
   Answer: AC = 130.54 m. AE = 94.61 m and E is at RL 108.562 m. */
