/* book exam traps: Using D = K s on an inclined sight. With a vertical staff it is K s cos²θ + C cos θ; leaving out cos²θ makes D too long by 2 % at 8°. | Taking C as zero for every instrument. It is zero only with an anallatic lens; external-focusing telescopes have about 0.3 to 0.45 m. | Setting the subtense bar along the line or at a slant in plan. It must be square to the line of sight, or the angle and the distance are too small. | Mixing signs in the tangential method. With one vane in depression, its angle is negative, and tan α₁ − tan α₂ becomes a sum of magnitudes. */
